In this lesson we'll look at how changing a single number inside a rule completely transforms the graph. The trick is to use a digital tool so you can move that number with a slider and watch the picture respond in real time, which makes patterns jump out that you'd never notice in a static sketch. Start by picking a family of graphs, like a basic parabola. Then hold everything fixed except one parameter, and slide it slowly up and down. Ask yourself, what is actually changing here, the shape, the position, or both? Once you have a hunch, like, as this number gets bigger the curve gets tighter, test it by trying several different values and seeing whether your hunch still holds. Finally, sum it up in one general rule that covers every example. For the classic parabola in completed square form, one number stretches or squashes the width, another slides it left and right, and another lifts it up or down. So really, you're doing detective work, turning a single rule into a whole visual story. Now let's see it in action.